==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|la|exasperō}}; {{m|la|ex|t=out of; thoroughly}} + {{m|la|asperō|t=make rough}}, from {{m|la|asper|t=rough}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪɡˈzæsp(ə)ɹeɪt/|a=RP,GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɪɡˈzɑːspəɹeɪt/|a=RP,also}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-exasperate.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æspəɹeɪt|s=4}}
* {{hyph|en|ex|as|per|ate}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[tax]] the patience of; [[irk]], [[frustrate]], [[vex]], [[provoke]], [[annoy]]; to make [[angry]].
#: {{syn|en|aggravate|rile|Thesaurus:annoy}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|3|6|text=And this report<br>Hath so '''exasperate''' [sic] the king that he<br>Prepares for some attempt of war.}}
#* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|chapter=3|text=The picture represents a Cape-Horner in a great hurricane; the half-foundered ship weltering there with its three dismantled masts alone visible; and an '''exasperated''' whale, purposing to spring clean over the craft, is in the enormous act of impaling himself upon the three mast-heads.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|chapter=11|text=Beadle goes into various shops and parlours, examining the inhabitants; always shutting the door first, and by exclusion, delay, and general idiotcy, '''exasperating''' the public.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=January 5 1987|title=Woman of the Year: Corazon Aquino|titleurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20111010004628/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,963185-9,00.html|journal=Time
|passage=[S]he '''exasperates''' her security men by acting as if she were protected by some invisible shield.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 4 2007|title=Loyal Mail|titleurl=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/comment/leading_article/article1878668.ece|journal=Times Online|accessdate=7 Oct 2010|location=UK
|passage=News that Adam Crozier, Royal Mail chief executive, is set to receive a bumper bonus will '''exasperate''' postal workers.}}

